------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream catalogues
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

redpen's rule corpus (rules/*.yaml) is a merge of four prior catalogues. Every
rule in the corpus carries a `sources:` list naming which of them described it
and under what identifier; run `redpen sources` for the rollup and `redpen why
<rule-id>` for a single rule's provenance.

The corpus is an independent reimplementation. Rule *concepts* — "violet reads
as a default", "gradient-filled headings are a tell" — were taken from these
catalogues with attribution. No rule text, detector code, or reference material
was copied from any of them; the detectors in src/channels/detectors.ts and the
severity weighting in rules/*.yaml are original work.

  impeccable
    https://github.com/pbakaus/impeccable
    Apache License 2.0
    Contributed the taxonomy behind 59 rendered-DOM detector concepts.

  hallmark
    https://github.com/nutlope/hallmark
    MIT License
    Contributed the 58-gate slop test and the named anti-pattern catalogue,
    including the genre exceptions recorded as `exceptSurfaces`.

  vibecoded-design-tells (unslop-ui)
    https://github.com/JCarterJohnson/vibecoded-design-tells
    MIT License (code); harvested Reddit text belongs to its original authors
    Contributed the only empirical ranking in the corpus. The `evidenceRank`
    and `weight` fields derive from that study's comment-level tabulation of
    3,033 comments drawn from 3.2M posts across 47 subreddits. Where a rule
    carries an evidenceRank, that number is the study's; where it does not, the
    weight is redpen's own judgement of consequence and is documented as such.

  frontend-design-audit
    https://github.com/mistyhx/frontend-design-audit
    MIT License
    Contributed the 15 usability heuristics and the fix-pattern library that
    the `usability` and `a11y` categories are built from.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What redpen added
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - The unified schema and the merge itself: 96 rules collapsed from 202
    upstream references, each with provenance retained.
  - Cross-channel promotion (src/promote.ts). No upstream catalogue reconciles
    a source-level hit against a rendered-DOM hit, so none of them can tell a
    confirmed defect from a regex guess.
  - Reddit-derived weighting applied across the whole corpus, so rules that
    humans actually name outrank rules a catalogue author felt strongly about.
  - Fingerprint memory (src/fingerprint/). Structural signatures compared
    across a project and across time, which is the only way to see the
    top-ranked complaint in the source data — that the pages all look the same.
